## The idea

Count each value. A count is between 1 and n, so make an array of **buckets indexed by count** and drop each value into its bucket. Then read buckets from the highest count down until you have k values.

## Step by step

1. Count with a dictionary.
2. buckets[count] = list of values with that count.
3. Walk counts from n down to 1; sort each bucket (tie rule: smaller first); take values until k.

## Worked example

**Input:** nums = [1,1,1,2,2,3], k = 2

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| counts | 1→3, 2→2, 3→1 |
| buckets | [3] = {1}, [2] = {2}, [1] = {3} |
| read from 6 down | take 1, then 2 → done |

**Result:** [1, 2]

## Cost

O(n) time and memory (bucket sort, no comparison sort over all values).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 347 — the k most frequent values, most frequent first; ties go to the smaller value.
public static class TopKFrequent {
    public static int[] Find(int[] nums, int k) {
        var count = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        foreach (int x in nums) count[x] = count.TryGetValue(x, out int c) ? c + 1 : 1;
        if (k < 0 || k > count.Count) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(k));

        // Bucket sort by frequency: a frequency is between 1 and n, so no comparison sort over all values.
        var buckets = new List<int>[nums.Length + 1];
        foreach (var pair in count) {
            if (buckets[pair.Value] == null) buckets[pair.Value] = new List<int>();
            buckets[pair.Value].Add(pair.Key);
        }
        var result = new List<int>(k);
        for (int f = nums.Length; f >= 1 && result.Count < k; f--) {   // highest frequency first
            if (buckets[f] == null) continue;
            buckets[f].Sort();                                          // the tie rule: smaller value first
            foreach (int x in buckets[f]) {
                if (result.Count == k) break;
                result.Add(x);
            }
        }
        return result.ToArray();
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Ties?**

Ask. Without a rule, the answer is not unique.

**Q: Complexity?**

Bucket sort O(n); a min-heap of size k is O(n log k) and also fine.

**Q: Counts arrive from many threads.**

ConcurrentDictionary.AddOrUpdate, or per-thread dictionaries merged at the end (faster).

**Q: An endless stream of search queries.**

Approximate: Count-Min Sketch + a heap of heavy hitters, or Space-Saving. Exact counts do not fit.

